    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d60">

                <pb facs="0433" n="219" sameAs="frus:frus1871/pg219"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">No. 47.</seg></opener>
                <head>Mr. Bailey to Mr. Davis.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 63.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">United States Consulate,
                                Hong-Kong</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1871-09-12"><hi rendition="#italic">September</hi> 12,
                        1871</date>. Received October 23.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> Referring to my dispatches Nos. 33 and 35,
                    I have the honor to inclose an official communication from the colonial
                    secretary of this colony, <pb facs="0434" n="220" xml:id="pg220"/> with
                    accompanying documents, concerning the shipment of coolies hence to the United
                    States by Mr. George E. Payne. A large proportion of the coolies referred to in
                    the registrar genera’s letter had undoubtedly been inveigled, and some
                    kidnapped, by the coolie broker Lai-On, who is, as I learn, a notorious Macao
                    dealer in human flesh. Fortunately, the coolies all escaped from the vessel,
                    (the Pacific mail-steamer “China,”) and are now at liberty.</p>
                <p>As I have frequently stated before, I repeat now, that the entire traffic is
                    top-full of fraud, corruption, and the most brutal inhumanity.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>DAVID H. BAILEY.</signed></closer>




